WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:05.633
Hello everyone!
This is the "Mathematical Experiments" channel.

00:00:05.633 --> 00:00:09.360
Today, I'm going to talk about geometric algebra.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.985
There is algebraic geometry - one of the most advanced fields in mathematics.

00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:21.567
And, there is also geometric algebra, which is something completely different,  although also a trendy thing with applications in physics.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:27.154
You can find many videos online about the geometric algebra.

00:00:27.154 --> 00:00:30.995
Title on the video "Swift Introduction"  has somewhat like definition of geometric product, but it is incorrect.

00:00:30.995 --> 00:00:33.778
But still, the video is good, I recommend it.

00:00:33.778 --> 00:00:37.805
These videos has pictures, animations, and beautiful examples.

00:00:37.805 --> 00:00:48.080
In particular, many of the videos provide this impressing example of how to write Maxwell's equations in one line using the geometric product.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.260
I will also try to get to this example in todays's video

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.986
And I want to pursue my way of telling about geometric algebra via Wolfram Mathematica (WM).

00:00:54.986 --> 00:01:00.000
As you might guess, I will show how to implement Clifford algebra in Wolfram Mathematica (WM).

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.620
I won't mislead you, this is not school material at all.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:10.971
And the complexity is primarily in the question:
"Why?"  or "What are all these constructs for?"

00:01:10.971 --> 00:01:18.248
Or ... maybe not. if you've heard about matrix determinants, vector products,

00:01:18.248 --> 00:01:24.789
if you know the concept of factorization, for example, what is a factor group.

00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:28.499
Or, if you know how to integrate over a surface,

00:01:28.499 --> 00:01:31.601
and familiar with differential forms.

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:40.691
THEN, you might see the reasoning behind these constructs.

00:01:40.691 --> 00:01:43.310
And, it may also be interesting for programmers,

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:51.849
who will learn how to define new operators or redefine existing ones in the Wolfram language.

00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:54.582
That's also an interesting topic.

00:01:54.582 --> 00:02:00.572
And the way you define operators in WM is completely different from how it's done in traditional programming languages, like  C++, Java, or Python.

00:02:00.572 --> 00:02:07.096
That's because Wolfram Mathematica is not an imperative programming language.

00:02:07.096 --> 00:02:11.858
It is a term rewriting system.

00:02:11.858 --> 00:02:14.438
It is based on rules on transformations of expressions.

00:02:14.438 --> 00:02:19.274
Literally, calculations in WM are not the execution of step-by-step instructions,

00:02:19.274 --> 00:02:23.885
but a step-by-step application of expression simplification rules.

00:02:23.885 --> 00:02:29.004
For example, this expression is simplified to the following.

00:02:29.004 --> 00:02:31.068
It is reduced to some expression that can not be reduced any further.

00:02:31.068 --> 00:02:34.823
So, b multiplied by 5 is reduced to  5 b.

00:02:34.823 --> 00:02:37.011
And this collapses to 32.

00:02:37.011 --> 00:02:38.276
Let's go.

00:02:38.276 --> 00:02:42.856
The first important concept is the concept of a multivector.

00:02:42.856 --> 00:02:46.721
Suppose we have some vector space.

00:02:46.721 --> 00:02:48.375
For example, three-dimensional.

00:02:48.375 --> 00:02:52.260
And it has basis vectors e₁, e₂, e₃.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.949
So we will write down these formal expressions:

00:02:56.949 --> 00:03:01.023
e₁ ^ e₂

00:03:01.023 --> 00:03:05.142
The " ^ " is the Wedge operator.
We will define some rules for this operator.

00:03:05.142 --> 00:03:07.598
In English, it is called Wedge. And it stands for special product, called exterior- or outer- product.

00:03:07.598 --> 00:03:13.022
When it is applied in a unary way,
it remains the word Wedge.

00:03:13.022 --> 00:03:18.038
And when it is applied to two operands or more,

00:03:18.038 --> 00:03:22.775
then it is displayed  as  ^.

00:03:22.775 --> 00:03:26.899
Nothing is clear yet, and it shouldn't be. Just follow me.

00:03:26.899 --> 00:03:31.268
In short, besides the vectors e₁, e₂, e₃,

00:03:31.268 --> 00:03:38.441
we will also consider  bivectors  e₁ ^ e₂ , e₂ ^ e₃ , etc

00:03:38.441 --> 00:03:40.559
And trivectors like e₁ ^ e₂ ^ e₃ .

00:03:40.559 --> 00:03:48.363
And we will also consider scalars - numbers, like 1, 2, -0.5, etc.

00:03:48.363 --> 00:03:58.215
In order to remove word Wedge
when Wedge is applied in a unary way,
we will add such a rule...

00:03:58.215 --> 00:04:02.016
Ok, here I probably need to draw some more examples...

00:04:07.544 --> 00:04:13.240
In general, one can write any combination of basic vectors connected by the wedge symbol " ^ "  and treat it as new basic vector.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:19.601
These are our new basic vectors.
Now we have an extended vector basis.

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:26.304
In addition to the vectors e₁, e₂, e₃,
we also have bivectors, trivectors, and so on.

00:04:26.304 --> 00:04:33.161
This is an basic vector multiplied by a given number.

00:04:33.161 --> 00:04:38.062
And this is a linear combination of
basic vectors multiplied by numbers.

00:04:38.062 --> 00:04:44.069
Here we have formal sum of a scalar, a vector, and a trivector.

00:04:44.069 --> 00:04:50.512
A regular vector is written in this way.
Its a vector with coordinates x, y, z.

00:04:50.512 --> 00:04:54.204
Well, we also have bivectors and function B3 for generating them.

00:04:54.204 --> 00:05:00.853
tAnd we have this thing P3. in three-dimensional space it is called pseudoscalar.

00:05:00.853 --> 00:05:10.461
In general, the space of ALL multivectors is
the space of linear combinations of these four expressions

00:05:10.461 --> 00:05:15.712
So, now we want to endow " ^ " operator with some properties.

00:05:15.712 --> 00:05:18.948
First, we want it to be like multiplication.

00:05:18.948 --> 00:05:22.962
Namely, we want to be able to expand these braces according to rule we learned in school – distributive low.

00:05:22.962 --> 00:05:29.600
Each element from one expression
 must be "multiplied" via " ^ " by each element from the second expression.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.654
To apply this distributive law,
WM has a Distribute function.

00:05:35.654 --> 00:05:41.728
It interprets the expression given inside as multiplication.

00:05:41.728 --> 00:05:47.838
That is, any function f,
it will treat as multiplication.

00:05:47.838 --> 00:05:52.172
And expand it, as if it were the multiplication of two expressions.

00:05:53.392 --> 00:05:57.514
Let's check that this works not only for f
 but also for " ^ ".

00:05:57.514 --> 00:05:59.488
Here, it works.

00:06:00.409 --> 00:06:04.550
We want this distributivity to be applied automatically.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:12.144
And we also want these numbers: 3, 13, to be pulled to the left and multiplied.

00:06:12.144 --> 00:06:20.731
So, from " ^ " we demand: associativity,
so that it doesn't matter how we arrange the braces.

00:06:20.731 --> 00:06:25.561
The distributivity with respect to addition,
as if " ^ " was multiplication.

00:06:25.561 --> 00:06:30.000
Numbers should be brought to the left, as a common factor.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:38.754
And also, " ^ " should be such that when multiplied by a number, that number is also brought to the left.

00:06:38.754 --> 00:06:44.018
And finally, the most important part about " ^ " is that it is anticommutative.

00:06:44.018 --> 00:06:54.008
So, when we have defined all that we have just defined, we have essentially defined the exterior Grassmann algebra.

00:06:54.008 --> 00:06:56.019
These are the laws.

00:06:56.941 --> 00:07:02.161
When we defined anticommutativity, what automatically appeared?

00:07:02.161 --> 00:07:05.957
What automatically appeared was that eᵢ ^ eᵢ == 0

00:07:05.957 --> 00:07:07.397
Why?

00:07:07.397 --> 00:07:14.897
Because... well, let's take i instead of j, move everything to one side,
 and we get 2 eᵢ ^ eᵢ == 0.

00:07:14.897 --> 00:07:17.726
This means that eᵢ ^ eᵢ == 0.

00:07:18.417 --> 00:07:25.904
It follows from the commutativity, we can't have two identical eᵢ
 in the product through " ^ ".

00:07:25.904 --> 00:07:29.444
We can, but the expression will be equal to zero.

00:07:29.444 --> 00:07:39.756
Therefore, the effective number of the basis vectors
 in the exterior Grassmann algebra over R³ is 8.

00:07:39.756 --> 00:07:41.592
We have scalars.

00:07:41.592 --> 00:07:46.253
We have three basis vectors from the usual vector space.

00:07:46.253 --> 00:07:50.000
Three bivectors and one trivector.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:54.446
Why is there only one? Why isn't there e₂ ^ e₁ ^ e₃?

00:07:54.446 --> 00:07:59.091
There is, but we can permute the elements

00:07:59.091 --> 00:08:06.768
and therefore, all combinations of permutations of the indices 1, 2, 3, ultimately reduce to this vector.

00:08:06.768 --> 00:08:11.746
By using permutation and bringing out the -1.

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:22.591
Let's consider the simplest implementation
 of the exterior algebra in WM in 3-dimensional space.

00:08:22.591 --> 00:08:28.714
This implementation literally fits on one screen.
With comments.

00:08:28.714 --> 00:08:35.004
Forget everything we knew about the Wedge operator and say that it is flat.

00:08:35.004 --> 00:08:40.356
This is how associativity is denoted in WM.

00:08:40.356 --> 00:08:43.629
Distributivity is done like this:

00:08:43.629 --> 00:08:57.589
We say that if we have Wedge of " a " multiplied by something and this " a " is an expression with a head plus, that is an expression where something plus something,

00:08:57.589 --> 00:09:01.507
then we must apply the Distribute function.

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:09.260
Let's temporarily denote Wedge as tmpV,
as such... some temporary symbol.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:17.764
We ask WM to interpret this symbol as multiplication and perform distribution
 with respect to addition.

00:09:18.185 --> 00:09:20.754
This is interpreted as multiplication.

00:09:20.754 --> 00:09:28.865
There are also two additional arguments in the Distribute function,
which specify what to replace plus with (with plus)

00:09:28.865 --> 00:09:32.535
and what to replace what we perceive as a multiplication with.

00:09:32.535 --> 00:09:34.474
The answer is Wedge.

00:09:34.474 --> 00:09:35.960
Next.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:44.423
If there is some prefix inside Wedge - some number of arguments...

00:09:44.423 --> 00:09:49.311
Three underscores mean some number of arguments: 0, 1 or more.

00:09:49.311 --> 00:09:51.919
That is a sequence of arguments.

00:09:51.919 --> 00:09:58.434
Then there is an argument that represents multiplying a number by something,

00:09:58.434 --> 00:10:01.667
and then another sequence of the arguments.

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:06.058
...so ultimately we have to simplify this expression to this,

00:10:06.058 --> 00:10:08.126
that is, to factor out the x multiplier from the expression.

00:10:09.766 --> 00:10:10.734
Next.

00:10:11.186 --> 00:10:17.140
If we encounter just a number, it should also be factored out of the expression.

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:21.619
And then the rule of anticommutativity goes:

00:10:21.619 --> 00:10:25.710
We say that eᵢ ^ eᵢ == 0...

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.616
Here, Wolfram understands such patterns.

00:10:27.616 --> 00:10:34.769
It understands that this index is any number,
but it should be the same number in both places.

00:10:35.972 --> 00:10:41.593
Next, we say that e₂ ^ e₁ should be turned into e₁ ^ e₂,

00:10:41.593 --> 00:10:45.080
e₃ ^ e₁ should be turned into e₁ ^ e₃,

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.569
and e₃ ^ e₂ should be turned into e₂ ^ e₃.

00:10:47.569 --> 00:10:57.235
So essentially what we're saying here is that when we search for neighbors
inside Wedge arguments with indices in the wrong order,

00:10:57.235 --> 00:11:00.784
we need to arrange those indices in the correct order.

00:11:00.784 --> 00:11:04.243
And the "-1" comes from anti-commutativity.

00:11:04.243 --> 00:11:06.307
Wedge[a_] is just equal to "a";

00:11:06.307 --> 00:11:13.908
We're saying here that the unary Wedge[a] is the argument "a" itself.

00:11:13.908 --> 00:11:17.833
And Wedge of nothing is equal to one:
Wedge[] := 1;

00:11:17.833 --> 00:11:21.457
In the end... everything works.

00:11:21.457 --> 00:11:28.163
And at this point, after a one-page definition,
we get something useful and applicable.

00:11:28.163 --> 00:11:36.996
We get that with the help of the wedge product,
we can write determinant of a matrix formed by three vectors.

00:11:36.996 --> 00:11:47.817
The wedge product of three vectors gives us a pseudoscalar with length equal to the determinant of the matrix.

00:11:49.809 --> 00:11:56.453
Let's learn more about attributes.

00:11:56.453 --> 00:11:59.805
For example, here's the built-in operator "+".

00:11:59.805 --> 00:12:06.442
It has the name "Plus",
And "a + b" is just the neat way to write Plus[a, b].

00:12:06.442 --> 00:12:12.070
So, the Plus operator has the following attributes.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:18.320
The first: it is Flat - which means that (a + b) + c,
 where the braces are placed like this,

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:23.223
becomes Plus of three arguments.

00:12:23.223 --> 00:12:29.648
In general, any hierarchy of Plus-es
becomes Plus with n arguments.

00:12:29.648 --> 00:12:31.662
That's what Flat means.

00:12:31.662 --> 00:12:34.514
And yes, it's not the associativity property.
It is not the definition of associativity property.

00:12:34.514 --> 00:12:38.710
It's about making the hierarchy one level deep.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:46.246
If we allow such flattening,
then the operator should be associative.

00:12:46.246 --> 00:12:54.393
Next. Listable is a convenient attribute
that allows to apply this function or operator directly to a list.

00:12:54.393 --> 00:12:59.592
A list plus a number is a list where each element has this number added to it.

00:12:59.592 --> 00:13:02.856
NumericFunction == number in then numbers out.

00:13:02.856 --> 00:13:10.664
OneIdentity means that the unary
Plus operator applied to x gives x.

00:13:10.664 --> 00:13:16.684
Orderless is an indication of commutativity.

00:13:16.684 --> 00:13:27.022
It means that Plus with any number of arguments
automatically becomes Plus with the sorted arguments.

00:13:27.022 --> 00:13:29.816
And yes, again, it's not commutativity itself.

00:13:29.816 --> 00:13:33.198
It's the transformation rule.

00:13:33.198 --> 00:13:39.160
And if the operator is commutative,
then we allow such transformation.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:44.681
Protected means that we cannot redefine this operator.

00:13:44.681 --> 00:13:46.609
Regarding the last one, it's simple.

00:13:46.609 --> 00:13:49.246
Imagine we want 1 + 2 to be equal to 4.

00:13:49.246 --> 00:13:50.575
Wolfram says No.

00:13:50.575 --> 00:13:58.978
But if we do Unprotect Plus,
then we can redefine the Plus operation for some cases.

00:13:58.978 --> 00:14:02.145
Let's step-by-step see how all this works.

00:14:02.145 --> 00:14:06.852
Let's consider some operator CirclePlus "⊕", for example.

00:14:09.261 --> 00:14:14.230
Currently, it doesn't know what to do with this expression
and no calculations are performed.

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:18.392
It doesn't have any rules about the CirclePlus operator.

00:14:18.392 --> 00:14:22.582
But as soon as we assign the Flat attribute,

00:14:22.582 --> 00:14:24.582
it starts performing some transformations.

00:14:24.582 --> 00:14:31.900
And the hierarchy of depth two is transformed into a single-level expression
 of the three arguments.

00:14:33.558 --> 00:14:35.262
Like this.

00:14:35.262 --> 00:14:39.724
If we set Orderless, then Wolfram automatically sorts arguments.

00:14:39.724 --> 00:14:40.822
See?

00:14:40.822 --> 00:14:42.908
It was 3 ⊕ 2 ⊕ 1, and now it's 1 ⊕ 2 ⊕ 3.

00:14:42.908 --> 00:14:52.293
And this allows it to know that these expressions are equal,
without performing any calculations.

00:14:55.333 --> 00:14:59.641
So CirclePlus[3].
It doesn't know what to do with this yet,

00:14:59.641 --> 00:15:03.003
but we can say that OneIdentity is fulfilled.

00:15:03.003 --> 00:15:07.200
Which means that the unary CirclePlus operator doesn't change its argument.

00:15:08.088 --> 00:15:12.080
But, truthfully, I found out that it doesn't quite understand this.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:17.597
If you want to explicitly make the transformation
of CirclePlus from " a " to " a ",

00:15:17.597 --> 00:15:21.025
then you need to write this rule.

00:15:21.025 --> 00:15:26.657
For complex reasons, you have to write Verbatim here,
I won't explain why.

00:15:26.657 --> 00:15:27.801
Here!

00:15:29.552 --> 00:15:39.117
Thus, if you want to define some properties of any operator,
partially it can be done via setting attributes.

00:15:39.117 --> 00:15:43.801
And part of it can be done with rules,

00:15:43.801 --> 00:15:48.247
like these.

00:15:48.247 --> 00:15:49.050
Next.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:52.247
I want to tell you a little bit about mathematics.

00:15:52.247 --> 00:15:55.630
About what tensor algebra, free algebra is.

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:57.881
And what factorization is.

00:15:59.447 --> 00:16:03.437
Tensor algebra is a very simple thing.

00:16:04.414 --> 00:16:11.865
And the main misunderstanding can arise with
 the question of why all of this is being done, this strange thing.

00:16:12.925 --> 00:16:16.118
So here it is.
Why is all of this being done?

00:16:16.634 --> 00:16:24.736
Tensor algebra is a raw blank thing,
from which you can later make whatever algebra you want.

00:16:25.185 --> 00:16:27.356
How it is constructed.

00:16:27.356 --> 00:16:34.702
Let's you have a number field K and some vector space over this field.

00:16:34.702 --> 00:16:38.564
And of course, there are operators for adding vectors.

00:16:38.564 --> 00:16:41.674
And multiplying vectors by numbers.

00:16:41.674 --> 00:16:43.275
What you do.

00:16:43.275 --> 00:16:48.536
You add an operator of tensor multiplication,

00:16:48.536 --> 00:16:54.588
which is associative, distributive,
 and linear with respect to multiplication by numbers.

00:16:54.588 --> 00:16:55.925
What does this mean?

00:16:55.925 --> 00:17:00.312
This means that it is not so important how the parentheses are arranged here.

00:17:00.312 --> 00:17:06.754
What needs to be done is to expand such expressions as if it were multiplication.

00:17:06.754 --> 00:17:08.728
I didn't write it here...

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:17.596
And that numbers can be taken out and multiplied with each other.

00:17:18.888 --> 00:17:23.912
Tensor algebra consists of expressions of this type.

00:17:23.912 --> 00:17:26.603
Sum. A formal sum of a scalar,

00:17:26.603 --> 00:17:29.335
some numbers multiplied by eᵢ.

00:17:29.335 --> 00:17:34.481
Some numbers multiplied by eᵢ ⊗ eⱼ, and so on.

00:17:34.481 --> 00:17:38.756
How to multiply two such formal sums?

00:17:38.756 --> 00:17:45.424
In very intuitive way. Each element from the first expression is multiplied by each element from the second expression.

00:17:45.424 --> 00:17:55.980
Then 3e₃⊗3e₁e₃ turns into 9 times e₃⊗e₁⊗e₃.

00:17:57.546 --> 00:17:58.561
Just like that.

00:17:59.344 --> 00:18:05.335
Two numbers 3 were factored out, multiplied,
and became a multiplier for this expression.

00:18:06.475 --> 00:18:08.885
This is recorded as follows:

00:18:08.885 --> 00:18:15.123
Tensor algebra over V denoted as T(V) is the direct infinite sum of spaces.

00:18:15.123 --> 00:18:19.666
The first is the number field K itself. The second is the original vector space V.

00:18:19.666 --> 00:18:26.422
then V² and V³ go and so on.

00:18:28.921 --> 00:18:32.112
For this sample formal sum we have

00:18:32.112 --> 00:18:35.308
2 belongs to the component K.

00:18:35.308 --> 00:18:38.571
This subexpression belongs to the component V.

00:18:38.571 --> 00:18:43.325
This bivector belongs to the component V², and so on.

00:18:43.325 --> 00:18:45.529
This is called grades.

00:18:45.529 --> 00:18:49.760
And all this stuff is just a formal sum,

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:53.395
constructed over eᵢ as if they were variables,

00:18:53.395 --> 00:18:57.245
and instead of multiplication we use sign "⊗". Which is not commutative in general.

00:18:57.245 --> 00:19:00.000
Just a formal sum.

00:19:02.101 --> 00:19:05.898
Sometimes it's hard to explain the concept of formal sums.

00:19:05.898 --> 00:19:09.207
People just starve for answer:

00:19:09.207 --> 00:19:12.451
What is the result of this tensor multiplication?

00:19:12.451 --> 00:19:15.630
It's difficult.

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:20.539
We don't expand this multiplication
because there is no answer to this question.

00:19:20.539 --> 00:19:21.822
Let's say...

00:19:22.513 --> 00:19:24.623
There is a joke like this:

00:19:25.821 --> 00:19:32.453
There are ten types of people:
those who understand binary arithmetic and those who don't.

00:19:32.453 --> 00:19:36.711
I think we can come up with a similar joke about formal sums:

00:19:36.711 --> 00:19:42.786
There are people who understand formal sums and
those who don't understand them,

00:19:42.786 --> 00:19:43.872
Or better like this:

00:19:43.872 --> 00:19:46.805
There are different types of people:

00:19:46.805 --> 00:19:49.770
those who don't care about formal sums,

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:52.258
those who don't understand them,

00:19:52.258 --> 00:19:53.872
those who understand them,

00:19:53.872 --> 00:19:57.460
those who don't understand them twice,

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:59.756
those who don't understand, but later understand,

00:19:59.756 --> 00:20:00.973
understand, and then don't understand,

00:20:00.973 --> 00:20:04.559
understand twice.

00:20:04.559 --> 00:20:09.250
And so people are divided into these total number of types.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:18.857
In summary:
Tensor algebra is an algebra of formal expressions constructed on vectors e₁, e₂, e₃, eₙ,

00:20:18.857 --> 00:20:26.750
where the multiplication sign looks like this.
It is associative, distributive, and numbers are factored to the left.

00:20:28.354 --> 00:20:29.342
Now...

00:20:30.547 --> 00:20:32.996
When we

00:20:32.996 --> 00:20:37.956
postulate the anti-commutativity of this multiplication,

00:20:37.956 --> 00:20:41.753
we immediately obtain the Grassmann algebra.

00:20:42.628 --> 00:20:46.881
And the moment we postulate this, we need to change tensor product symbol to this wedge symbol

00:20:46.881 --> 00:20:47.848
And that's it!

00:20:48.815 --> 00:20:53.238
On a mathematical language, it is written as follows:

00:20:53.238 --> 00:21:01.382
The exterior product of a vector space with itself is the tensor product V ⊗ V,

00:21:01.382 --> 00:21:06.055
factored by expressions of the form v ⊗ v.

00:21:06.055 --> 00:21:14.358
In other words, we are saying that two elements inside the tensor algebra V ⊗ V are considered the same,

00:21:14.358 --> 00:21:19.352
if they do differ by v ⊗ v.

00:21:20.642 --> 00:21:23.604
Or an equivalent definition of the exterior algebra.

00:21:24.894 --> 00:21:25.724
Once again.

00:21:26.922 --> 00:21:34.658
The exterior algebra over the vector space V is the tensor algebra T(V).
So it ingerits from  T(V) all its properties.

00:21:34.658 --> 00:21:39.008
Then we replace the tensor multiplication symbol with Wedge.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:42.185
This replacement is the most important thing, apparently.

00:21:42.185 --> 00:21:51.161
We also postulated anti-commutativity when we take the exterior multiplication of two vectors from V.

00:21:51.161 --> 00:21:54.312
Or two basis vectors from V.

00:21:56.569 --> 00:21:58.010
So...

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:01.504
The formal sum looks the same as before.

00:22:01.504 --> 00:22:04.676
But there are some new aspects in the multiplication of expressions.

00:22:05.091 --> 00:22:08.151
Let's take a look at this.

00:22:08.151 --> 00:22:10.584
And what will be in the end?

00:22:10.584 --> 00:22:11.783
In the end, it will be...

00:22:14.378 --> 00:22:16.874
Well, it seems like I have some errors here ...

00:22:20.329 --> 00:22:23.226
Here we have 1 and 3

00:22:25.108 --> 00:22:32.841
So, in the end, we have 1 multiplied by each element in this expression, resulting in this.

00:22:32.841 --> 00:22:38.454
-2e₂ multiplied by each element in this expression results in this.

00:22:38.454 --> 00:22:41.534
And 3e₃ does the same.

00:22:42.087 --> 00:22:44.032
What do we do next?

00:22:44.032 --> 00:22:50.000
Next, we see that this equals zero because e₂ ^ e₂ = 0.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:56.901
Next, here we order the indices and swap e₂ and e₁.

00:22:56.901 --> 00:23:04.311
We mentally draw brackets around them, swap them, a minus sign appears, which cancels out the previous minus sign.

00:23:04.311 --> 00:23:06.357
Resulting in 6.

00:23:08.799 --> 00:23:16.980
And here this term disappears because e₃ ^ e₃ appears in it.

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:20.595
How can we prove that this expression equals zero?

00:23:20.595 --> 00:23:25.359
First, we draw brackets around it, swap them, the minus sign appears,

00:23:25.359 --> 00:23:27.359
and now e₃ is here.

00:23:27.359 --> 00:23:34.054
Then we draw brackets around e₃ ^ e₃ and it equals zero,

00:23:34.054 --> 00:23:39.077
the zero flies out as a common factor, so this term is also eliminated.

00:23:40.828 --> 00:23:42.183
That's how it is.

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:51.811
A small addition about anticommutativity and from the general blank, tensor algebra, we obtained the exterior algebra of Grassmann.

00:23:51.811 --> 00:23:58.173
And here we have this interesting result that the exterior product of three vectors

00:23:58.173 --> 00:24:01.576
gives us a pseudovector with a determinant in front of it.

00:24:01.576 --> 00:24:10.391
Clifford algebra is constructed similarly to factorization over T(V).

00:24:10.391 --> 00:24:13.643
There is a wrong approach to defining Clifford algebra.

00:24:13.643 --> 00:24:15.326
Like this.

00:24:15.326 --> 00:24:21.451
Let's define the operator " ∘ " (geometric product)
using " ^ " and " · ".

00:24:21.451 --> 00:24:24.350
And a dot " · " represents a scalar product.

00:24:24.350 --> 00:24:29.176
But no. This is the wrong approach.
This expression cannot be the definition.

00:24:29.176 --> 00:24:32.465
This expression is true in specific cases.

00:24:32.465 --> 00:24:34.921
It's like a consequence for some specific cases.

00:24:34.921 --> 00:24:39.106
And the definition of Clifford algebra is as follows:

00:24:39.106 --> 00:24:42.845
We take the tensor algebra T(V) again,

00:24:42.845 --> 00:24:49.129
and postulate that for any vector " a " in V,

00:24:49.129 --> 00:24:58.785
" a ∘ a " gives us a number, a scalar, equal to the
quadratic form of the vector " a ".

00:24:58.785 --> 00:25:02.076
Usually, the ordinary form is taken as the quadratic form,

00:25:02.076 --> 00:25:11.014
which returns the number 1 for all unit vectors in space V.

00:25:11.014 --> 00:25:12.159
Or in other words:

00:25:12.159 --> 00:25:18.766
Clifford algebra is the factorization of the algebra T(V)
over the set of such expressions.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:24.711
The words may be unclear, but what to do in practice
is very understandable.

00:25:24.711 --> 00:25:25.838
I'll show you now.

00:25:26.391 --> 00:25:31.221
So, let our quadratic form Q be equal to 1
for our unit vectors.

00:25:31.221 --> 00:25:36.722
Then eᵢ ∘ eᵢ - Q(eᵢ) should be equal to zero.

00:25:36.722 --> 00:25:40.798
What does this mean?
This means that eᵢ ∘ eᵢ = 1.

00:25:41.628 --> 00:25:43.419
Okay.

00:25:43.419 --> 00:25:50.267
Next, let's write this expression,
which should be equal to zero, for eᵢ + eⱼ.

00:25:50.267 --> 00:25:52.267
This is what we will get.

00:25:53.188 --> 00:25:55.450
Moving Q to the other side.

00:25:55.450 --> 00:26:01.240
Understanding that the square of the length of this vector is 2.

00:26:01.240 --> 00:26:06.324
This is the square of the hypotenuse in
a right triangle with legs of length 1, 1.

00:26:07.061 --> 00:26:09.521
As a result, we get...

00:26:09.521 --> 00:26:13.153
We expand these brackets according to the distributive law.

00:26:13.153 --> 00:26:16.014
We get this expression.

00:26:16.014 --> 00:26:22.529
We set it equal to 2 and obtain the anticommutativity of our
basic vectors.

00:26:22.529 --> 00:26:25.956
You see:
we postulated this,

00:26:25.956 --> 00:26:28.787
and as a consequence, we obtained anticommutativity.

00:26:28.787 --> 00:26:30.971
Just like in exterior algebra.

00:26:30.971 --> 00:26:34.555
But in exterior algebra, eᵢ ^ eᵢ = 0.

00:26:34.555 --> 00:26:39.432
While for us, eᵢ ∘ eᵢ = 1.

00:26:39.432 --> 00:26:42.952
That is, anticommutativity holds for different unit vectors,

00:26:42.952 --> 00:26:51.971
and unity holds for the square of one specific vector.

00:26:51.971 --> 00:26:55.912
In the end, we have the following thing:

00:26:55.912 --> 00:26:58.438
let's say we have such a long expression,

00:26:58.438 --> 00:27:05.045
where through the circle are multiplied some unit
vectors from the original space V.

00:27:05.045 --> 00:27:11.015
Then with the help of such permutations of adjacent elements,

00:27:11.015 --> 00:27:18.539
we can move any of these vectors in any direction.

00:27:18.539 --> 00:27:23.990
And in particular, if we have two identical factors, eᵢ and eᵢ here too,

00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:29.472
then we can move eᵢ closer in some number of steps.

00:27:29.472 --> 00:27:36.790
When we connect them and draw them side by side,
 we can draw brackets around them

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:41.139
and perform this multiplication according to this rule.

00:27:41.139 --> 00:27:49.240
As a result, we can collapse identical eᵢ in this product.

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:58.677
In the end, our effective basis consists only of
 products of different eᵢ.

00:27:58.677 --> 00:28:07.864
Just like in exterior algebra, we end up
with 8 basic vectors in this tensor algebra.

00:28:13.162 --> 00:28:14.938
Let's move on.

00:28:14.938 --> 00:28:18.601
Here I just showed that it's possible to redefine multiplication,

00:28:18.601 --> 00:28:20.759
the star operator " * ",

00:28:20.759 --> 00:28:22.846
for example, like this:

00:28:22.846 --> 00:28:28.009
by introducing the special letter tau " τ " to represent unit vectors...

00:28:28.009 --> 00:28:32.885
I didn't use " e " here to avoid spoiling " e ".

00:28:32.885 --> 00:28:40.900
...and saying that the multiplication of two vectors equals 1 if i=j, and 0 otherwise.

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:49.738
So this thing allows us to immediately
get the scalar (inner) product of vectors.

00:28:49.738 --> 00:28:55.054
Okay. A lot of time has passed and I haven't told you anything interesting yet.

00:28:56.406 --> 00:29:05.932
Here I carefully define the exterior algebra and
define the Wedge operator for the exterior product

00:29:05.932 --> 00:29:10.691
and the CenterDot operator for the scalar (inner) product.

00:29:10.691 --> 00:29:13.027
A lot of things are repeated here.

00:29:13.027 --> 00:29:18.488
In fact, I repeat all the same things that we had.

00:29:18.488 --> 00:29:22.112
I make convenient functions to generate:

00:29:22.112 --> 00:29:27.524
bivectors or just vectors, or pseudovectors
of the general type.

00:29:29.437 --> 00:29:32.829
Here's the definition you've already seen.

00:29:32.829 --> 00:29:34.868
I just repeat it here.

00:29:34.868 --> 00:29:40.794
CenterDot, the scalar (inner) product, is defined in a similar way.

00:29:40.794 --> 00:29:47.541
Everything is the same. Distributivity. The ability to pull numbers out of brackets.

00:29:47.541 --> 00:29:51.360
But a little different here.

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:55.379
Scalar (inner) product with a number always gives 0.

00:29:55.379 --> 00:29:58.589
The unary operator CentorDot gives 0.

00:29:58.589 --> 00:30:03.560
The operator applied to an empty set also gives zero.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.890
And it is also postulated that it is orthogonal.

00:30:09.272 --> 00:30:16.287
Here you might wonder: why does e₁ • e₁ equal 1 when this rule exists?

00:30:16.287 --> 00:30:18.239
The answer is complicated.

00:30:18.239 --> 00:30:22.340
This rule takes precedence because it is simpler.

00:30:22.340 --> 00:30:24.817
I won't go into detail.

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:34.890
The scalar (inner) product is understood as being constructed in grade 1, within the space V.

00:30:34.890 --> 00:30:39.304
But how do we define it in V² or V³?

00:30:39.304 --> 00:30:48.512
The answer is: however you like,
but it is usually accepted to define the scalar (inner) product in this way.

00:30:48.512 --> 00:30:56.219
The scalar product of a bivector with itself is -1.

00:30:56.909 --> 00:31:00.294
The scalar product of a unit bivector with itself is -1.

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:05.024
The scalar product of a trivector with itself is also -1.

00:31:05.024 --> 00:31:07.867
The scalar product of a four-vector with itself is 1.

00:31:07.867 --> 00:31:11.373
There such rule is used...

00:31:11.373 --> 00:31:15.794
You need to understand it in your mind like this:

00:31:15.794 --> 00:31:19.336
Let's move this eₖ to the left twice.

00:31:19.336 --> 00:31:20.789
Moved.

00:31:20.789 --> 00:31:24.061
And we have eₖ here and eₖ here

00:31:24.061 --> 00:31:27.779
and we mentally draw brackets here and get 1.

00:31:27.779 --> 00:31:31.185
And our eₖ annihilates itself.

00:31:31.185 --> 00:31:34.418
Then we move eⱼ to the left.

00:31:34.418 --> 00:31:39.213
And again eⱼ • eⱼ annihilates each other.

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:44.759
In short, practice permutation in your mind here

00:31:44.759 --> 00:31:54.474
and when two vectors are close to each other through a dot, they annihilate each other if it is the same unit vector eᵢ.

00:31:54.474 --> 00:31:55.546
Next.

00:31:55.546 --> 00:32:03.513
The scalar product of a vector from V and a vector from V² works like this.

00:32:03.513 --> 00:32:09.440
eᵢ appears to collapse with eᵢ, resulting in 1. Only eₖ remains.

00:32:09.440 --> 00:32:16.638
And here, in order to collapse eᵢ with this eᵢ, we need to first rearrange the elements inside the bracket, and the -1 will come out of the bracket.

00:32:16.638 --> 00:32:19.207
In short, this is probably all complicated,

00:32:19.207 --> 00:32:25.429
but this is how we need to correctly define the scalar product.

00:32:28.162 --> 00:32:34.843
In principle, instead of a set of rules, we could write one rule in a general form.

00:32:34.843 --> 00:32:37.015
Multiplying eⱼ, eₖ, and eᵢ

00:32:37.015 --> 00:32:42.319
and writing that j is equal to k and what if is not equal, etc.

00:32:43.686 --> 00:32:46.655
We check that all this works...

00:32:50.936 --> 00:32:55.178
So, something didn't work here, we'll fix it now...

00:32:56.232 --> 00:33:01.009
I just didn't execute this block. We continue checking...

00:33:02.435 --> 00:33:04.798
We execute: this is 0,

00:33:04.798 --> 00:33:06.993
this is -1,

00:33:06.993 --> 00:33:08.993
this is -e₃.

00:33:08.993 --> 00:33:14.625
e₁ ^ e₂ collapsed with e₁ ^ e₂ and gave -1

00:33:14.625 --> 00:33:18.471
Because this is -1.

00:33:18.471 --> 00:33:21.024
And this gives e₃.

00:33:21.849 --> 00:33:24.900
Because first we move e₃ twice to the end,

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:27.743
then e₂ annihilates and e₁ annihilates.

00:33:28.953 --> 00:33:34.189
These definitions could be given through the signature of the permutation.

00:33:36.323 --> 00:33:38.457
For example, let's look here.

00:33:38.457 --> 00:33:40.887
We could do the following:

00:33:40.887 --> 00:33:44.917
The outer product eᵢ ^ eⱼ ^ eₖ is equal to...

00:33:46.139 --> 00:33:52.437
If all of these indices are different, that is, i, j, k are 1, 2, 3 in some order.

00:33:52.437 --> 00:33:59.494
This product should be equal to e₁ ^ e₂ ^ e₃ multiplied by 1 or -1,

00:33:59.494 --> 00:34:05.366
depending on how many permutations
of adjacent elements are needed to obtain 1, 2, 3.

00:34:05.366 --> 00:34:07.975
This is called the signature of a permutation.

00:34:07.975 --> 00:34:10.545
But here we encounter an unpleasant surprise:

00:34:10.545 --> 00:34:19.183
When we write down this rule, we get e₁ ^ e₂ ^ e₃ on the right,
and this rule is applied to that expression again.

00:34:19.183 --> 00:34:22.114
This results in infinite recursion.

00:34:22.114 --> 00:34:28.589
To avoid this, we need to write something on the right-hand side
that doesn't fit this pattern.

00:34:28.589 --> 00:34:34.321
In some packages, this is done as follows:

00:34:34.321 --> 00:34:41.459
This product is written as " e " with a set of indices,
where the set of indices is already ordered.

00:34:43.833 --> 00:34:48.353
This is one possible approach, but I didn't go down this path.

00:34:49.095 --> 00:34:52.306
So, let's check that everything is working...

00:34:52.306 --> 00:34:54.852
The numbers are outside the brackets.

00:34:54.852 --> 00:35:00.741
But there's a problem with x.
It doesn't know that x is a number. It could be a vector too.

00:35:00.741 --> 00:35:09.480
We would like it to eventually realize that x is a number
 and take it outside the brackets.

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:11.427
We'll do that now.

00:35:11.427 --> 00:35:13.700
Here's how it's done.

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:23.776
So you see, it calculates the determinant of the matrix, but if we add
abstract symbolic coordinates x1, y1, z1, etc.,

00:35:23.776 --> 00:35:28.207
we won't get the determinant formula, but we want to.

00:35:28.207 --> 00:35:35.618
To do this, instead of defining it like this,

00:35:35.618 --> 00:35:44.133
where we require x to be a number and then take x outside the brackets,
we will require x to be a non-vector.

00:35:45.305 --> 00:35:48.223
And we will take the non-vector outside the brackets.

00:35:48.223 --> 00:35:50.000
Here's how it's done...

00:35:52.928 --> 00:36:04.223
We say that a vector is an expression that begins with CenterDot or
an expression that begins with Wedge or the basic vector e.

00:36:05.590 --> 00:36:10.989
And everything else, Except[VectorT], is not a vector.

00:36:10.989 --> 00:36:16.291
Yes, and we also say
that an expression that begins with Plus is also not a vector.

00:36:16.291 --> 00:36:21.750
We want Plus to be handled through the distributive law

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:27.807
and therefore do not consider sums as non-vectors.

00:36:29.798 --> 00:36:33.281
We check what matches what

00:36:33.281 --> 00:36:42.505
and see that e₃ is a vector and NOT a non-vector.

00:36:42.505 --> 00:36:45.484
But this expression is kind of meaningless.

00:36:45.484 --> 00:36:51.648
It is neither an expression of type VectorT nor of type NVectorT.

00:36:53.913 --> 00:36:55.901
Next.

00:36:55.901 --> 00:37:00.348
I define CenterDot,

00:37:00.348 --> 00:37:04.999
now using NVectorT instead of Q-number.

00:37:06.277 --> 00:37:08.180
Well, as I already said.

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:13.931
We check:
 " 123 " is taken out of the bracket and " abc " is also taken out of the bracket.

00:37:15.298 --> 00:37:19.507
We check...

00:37:19.507 --> 00:37:26.880
It considers this a non-vector,
 and when a vector is scalar multiplied by a non-vector, it always equals zero.

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:34.878
So we postulated here. There is no secret.

00:37:34.878 --> 00:37:35.378
Next.

00:37:35.378 --> 00:37:43.019
We want it to collect identical terms while doing symbolic computations.

00:37:43.019 --> 00:37:46.643
Like here, for example, where 1+a is collected when x is used.

00:37:46.643 --> 00:37:57.390
We want the same thing to happen in this expression,

00:37:57.390 --> 00:38:02.123
so that these two terms are combined into one term.

00:38:02.123 --> 00:38:10.969
To do this, we define the function tE,
 which performs a Collect operation on the expression.

00:38:10.969 --> 00:38:17.186
And the second argument of Collect is the patterns that should be treated as variables.

00:38:17.186 --> 00:38:25.793
We are saying something like:
we need to collect all expressions with these variables.

00:38:25.793 --> 00:38:27.938
Let's check...

00:38:27.938 --> 00:38:41.066
Here is an important milestone: using Grassmann algebra, we obtained a symbolic expression for the determinant of a 3x3 matrix.

00:38:43.356 --> 00:38:48.765
Let's check that everything works as expected.

00:38:48.765 --> 00:38:53.809
Here, the product of this and this gives us this.

00:38:53.809 --> 00:38:59.393
Because the product of this and this is zero,

00:38:59.393 --> 00:39:01.556
and only this part remains.

00:39:02.101 --> 00:39:04.312
Good. Everything is correct.

00:39:04.312 --> 00:39:10.548
Another way to obtain a symbolic expression for the determinant is to perform a mixed product of three vectors.

00:39:10.548 --> 00:39:20.515
Here it is transformed into an outer product of two vectors multiplied by a bivector.

00:39:21.575 --> 00:39:23.699
What else I wanted to tell you here.

00:39:23.699 --> 00:39:29.175
I wanted to tell you how you can leave some things until the end.

00:39:29.175 --> 00:39:36.393
That is, some laws, transformations,
you write them using " := ",

00:39:36.393 --> 00:39:43.042
and some can be postponed until the very end,
at the time of executing the tE function.

00:39:43.042 --> 00:39:50.570
In this function, there is a hidden Collect operation and also, for example,
some rules for simplification.

00:39:50.570 --> 00:40:00.273
Here I use this arrow, not that one,
because I want the right-hand side computations

00:40:00.273 --> 00:40:03.185
to be performed each time,

00:40:03.185 --> 00:40:11.220
and not that we first memorize these rules, compute the right-hand side and then substitute the computed value.

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:14.745
No. We will calculate the right-hand side in the rules every time.

00:40:14.745 --> 00:40:18.350
The difference between these two arrows is well visible in these two examples.

00:40:21.465 --> 00:40:26.673
I have to say that I don't really need this, it all works without it.

00:40:26.673 --> 00:40:31.851
I just didn't get it to work at first and I thought it would be useful.

00:40:31.851 --> 00:40:37.787
In short, some final transformation rules can be left until the last stage.

00:40:38.544 --> 00:40:42.433
Now let's define the Clifford algebra.

00:40:42.433 --> 00:40:47.724
Using the SmallCircle symbol "∘".

00:40:47.724 --> 00:40:49.724
I'll say it again,

00:40:49.724 --> 00:40:57.298
there is a temptation to define SmallCircle
using the dot symbol "•" and the exterior product "^",

00:40:57.298 --> 00:41:00.277
but it doesn't work.

00:41:00.277 --> 00:41:03.108
It works, but only for small grades.

00:41:03.108 --> 00:41:09.208
Therefore, for SmallCircle, for geometric product,
we need to start from scratch.

00:41:09.208 --> 00:41:16.959
Now we will use SmallCircle to create multivectors.

00:41:16.959 --> 00:41:20.196
We write that SmallCircle[v_] := v;

00:41:20.196 --> 00:41:26.413
Yes, and here I tried not to make the attribute Flat,
but to define the concept of Flat myself.

