00:47:39.288 --> 00:47:43.904
Well, more precisely, there is only one e₁ ∘ e₂.

00:47:43.904 --> 00:47:50.000
e₂ ∘ e₁ also exists, but it is equal to - e₁ ∘ e₂.

00:47:50.000 --> 00:48:02.280
That is, in the Clifford algebra Cl₂,₀(R), we could consider a space consisting of a linear combination of a scalar and a bivector.

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:09.772
We only consider bivectors and scalars, and then in this space, we get complex numbers.

00:48:10.514 --> 00:48:12.698
